SPU: a trampoline step costs half as much (6.5 -> 3.4 ns): the per-step hooks check their switches before calling.

Lifted SPU code moves between its functions through the trampoline: each step returns to the drain loop
(spu_drain_call, or SPU_DRAIN at the top level), which ran spu_task_launch_check, yz_lockstep_tick and, through
the first, spu_check_stack_reset. In a Resistance 3 firefight these took ~15% of the SPU threads' time (`sample`:
spu_task_launch_check 755-833 samples of ~12.4k, spu_check_stack_reset ~200, yz_lockstep_tick ~130, plus their
_tlv_get_addr calls), though with no debug variable set they do little:
- spu_task_launch_check keeps the LS-0 job end and the PM flow trace inline; the variables' paths (SPU_NO_LS0_END,
  SPU_EXITTRACE, SPU_STEPTRACE, SPU_JOBDRAIN) and the job end itself go to a cold function, the old body. Its
  register spills for those paths were paid at every step.
- its ring of recent PCs is one thread-local object instead of two (on Darwin each access is a call);
- spu_check_stack_reset looks at its (global) entry count before the thread-local halt flag;
- yz_lockstep_tick is called only while lockstep is on; SPU_DRAIN asks spu_tailret_enabled() only with a host
  frame open (host_depth > 0, the condition it already required);
- spu_depth_guard (every lifted call) wrote a ring shared by all SPU threads at each call; it records only within
  the ring's length of the limit, which still holds the last sites before a trip (the depth grows by at most one
  per drain).

Test: runtime/spu/tests/test_spu_lifted_start.c, test_job_end_at_ls0 (a job branching to LS 0 halts there, at
the top level and in a nested drain; a first step at 0 runs; a policy module's 0 is not an end; the PM flow trace
sees each step): 183 checks pass, the same with the old runtime; skipping the job end or the trace fails them.
Micro-benchmark (a function re-queued 4M times through spu_drain_call): 6.2-6.8 -> 3.4 ns per step, 1.6 ns
without spu_task_launch_check at all.
In the game (`sample`, 25 s of the Haven firefight, 2 runs each): the step hooks (spu_task_launch_check,
spu_check_stack_reset, yz_lockstep_tick, spu_depth_guard, spu_drain_call, spu_indirect_branch, _tlv_get_addr)
14-17% -> ~10% of the SPU threads' samples; spu_task_launch_check 553/639 -> 193/184, yz_lockstep_tick gone.

Upstream: not proposed yet.
